# HackTheBox — Base

**Difficulty:** Easy
**OS:** Linux
**Target:** 10.129.47.59

## Summary

Base is an easy Linux machine centered around a static Bootstrap-template website with a hidden login portal. Initial access is gained by exploiting a PHP type-juggling flaw in a `strcmp()` authentication check, discovered by reading a leftover Vim swap file. The login bypass leads to a file upload feature, which is abused to drop a PHP webshell. Enumeration via the webshell uncovers hardcoded admin credentials in a config file, which are reused for SSH access as a real system user. Privilege escalation to root is achieved through a sudo misconfiguration allowing `find` to be run as root, abused via GTFOBins to spawn a root shell.

---

## Recon

### Nmap

```
nmap -sC -sV 10.129.47.59
```

```
PORT   STATE SERVICE VERSION
22/tcp open  ssh     OpenSSH 7.6p1 Ubuntu 4ubuntu0.7 (Ubuntu Linux; protocol 2.0)
80/tcp open  http    Apache httpd 2.4.29 ((Ubuntu))
|_http-title: Welcome to Base
```

Two open ports: SSH (noted for later, once credentials are found) and HTTP, serving a site titled "Base."

### Web enumeration

The site (`base.htb`) is a static one-page Bootstrap template. Every nav link (pricing, services, team, etc.) scrolls to a section on the same page rather than loading new content — there's no server-side functionality behind most of it.

Two areas stood out as interactive:
- A **contact form**, which on submission always returned: *"There was an issue sending your message. Please call us instead."*
- A **login page**.

Browsing to `/assets/` revealed Apache was serving a **directory listing** (misconfigured `Options Indexes`), exposing the site's raw CSS/JS/image source tree.

Checking `/forms/` also returned a directory listing, containing a `Readme.txt`:

```
Fully working PHP/AJAX contact form script is available in the pro version of the template.
You can buy it from: https://bootstrapmade.com/onepage-multipurpose-bootstrap-template/
```

This confirmed the contact form was a stub from a paid template — never fully implemented — explaining the earlier error message, and suggesting the site was deployed somewhat carelessly.

### Credential brute force (abandoned)

An initial attempt was made to brute-force the login form with `hydra`, using a custom username list built from names found on the site's "Team" section, against `rockyou.txt`:

```
hydra -L base_users.txt -P /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt 10.129.47.59 http-post-form \
  "/login/login.php:username=^USER^&password=^PASS^:F=Wrong Username or Password"
```

This approach was far too slow (143M+ combinations) and was abandoned in favor of directly auditing the login mechanism instead.

